1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:03,150
Cloud Gaming in 2026.

2
00:00:03,100 --> 00:00:15,825
Cloud gaming has reached mainstream viability in 2026, with Microsoft's Xbox Cloud Gaming (xCloud), Nvidia's GeForce Now, and Sony's PlayStation Cloud Streaming leading the charge.

3
00:00:15,825 --> 00:00:27,587
xCloud now supports over 4,000 titles across mobile, PC, and smart TVs, with Microsoft reporting xCloud users now stream more than 100 million hours per month.

4
00:00:27,587 --> 00:00:43,587
GeForce Now, meanwhile, has pushed technical boundaries with its RTX 5090-powered servers, enabling 4K at 120 frames per second with full ray tracing on virtually any device — including smartphones and low-end laptops.

5
00:00:43,587 --> 00:00:52,812
Analysts estimate the cloud gaming market has surpassed $4 billion in annual revenue, though latency remains a challenge in rural and developing regions.

6
00:00:52,812 --> 00:01:00,300
Major carriers in the US and Europe have begun offering cloud gaming data bundles, treating it similarly to video streaming.

7
00:01:00,300 --> 00:01:08,575
The technology has been particularly transformative in markets like Brazil and India, where high console prices have historically limited access.

